/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  solvent01 -- DIDACTIC EXAMPLE WITH SYNTHETIC PARAMETERS (DEV.md 4c C128,
  adjustment 2: start with a synthetic case, then a real one, then the
  confrontation with measurements).

  THE ARITHMETIC, by hand (MPa^0.5):

      solventA   dD 16   dP  2   dH  4
      solventB   dD 16   dP 10   dH 12
      polymerP   dD 17   dP  6   dH  8     R0 4

  Along the A/B line at volume fraction s of B the mixture rule gives
  (16, 2 + 8 s, 4 + 8 s), so

      Ra^2 = 4 (16 - 17)^2 + (8 s - 4)^2 + (8 s - 4)^2 = 4 + 2 (8 s - 4)^2

  which is 36 at either pure end (Ra = 6.000, RED = 1.5) and 4 at s = 0.5
  (Ra = 2.000, RED = 0.5): the minimum sits at 50:50 BY CONSTRUCTION, and
  the dispersion offset of 1 keeps it from being a degenerate zero.  The two
  solvents have equal Vliq, so the volume, mole and mass bases coincide
  here -- the basis conversion is exercised on the real case instead.

  THE PHASE WORD IS `notVerified`, honestly: the package declares an ideal
  liquid (gamma = 1), which has no excess Gibbs energy and can never demix,
  so a stability test on it would say "homogeneous" about every pair ever
  asked.  The op marks every blend point rather than claim a verification
  the model cannot give.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name       blend;
        type       hansenMixture;
        target     polymerP;                 // a record carrying hansen { ... R0 }
        solvents   ( solventA solventB );
        composition
        {
            basis  volume;                   // the fraction of solventB, in volume
            n      21;                       // pure A ... pure B, 0.05 apart
        }
        T          298.15 K;                 // the parameters' Tref; used for the phase check
        output     { file "hansenMixture.csv"; }
    }
);
